From 3ae7d003092a3746137784e5434bebeb4d060600 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:53:44 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Stereo depth: the wallpaper keeps its size and is cut at
 the sides

The wallpaper is the deepest layer, behind the screen at the sunk limit,
and Stereo KWin never stretches an eye: it scales once, at the output.
46ed9c62d5 had the desktop window drawn wider by what each eye's copy
moves, (W + 2 * ((|p| + 1) / 2)) / W about its middle, so that the moved
copy still reached both edges in each eye; for a stereo wallpaper that
stretched each eye horizontally (by 0.625 % at 1280 wide with the default
Background depth of 10) and scaled the content's own disparity with it.

The desktop window now only moves by its eye's shift, and the columns
either eye's copy leaves open are cut from both sides, the same columns
in both eyes: (|p| + 1) / 2 per side, as many as the farther copy moves
(StereoDepth::wallpaperCut()). That is half the level's parallax rounded
up, read from the user's Background depth and each output's own width as
the parallax already is: 5 columns at 1920 wide with the default, 4 at
1280. The wallpaper's own fill mode still lays the picture over the whole
output, so what is left is covered in both eyes. The depth picture of the
L + depth outputs and the shadows that fall on the desktop follow the
same place. With a Background depth of 0, and on 2D outputs, nothing is
moved and nothing is cut.

testStereoDepthWallpaper draws a desktop whose columns each have a
colour of their own and checks every column of both eyes: the wallpaper's
own column moved by the eye's shift, black in the cut columns, at an even
and an odd parallax, with no sunk limit, and on a 2D output.

